// =============================================================================
// handshake.fbs — connection establishment.
//
// Always first on a connection, and from V7 it is more than one exchange.
//
// Through V6 it was exactly one: the client sent ClientHello and the server
// answered with ServerWelcome or ServerReject. From V7 the client presents a
// signed session ticket and then chooses which of its characters is playing, so
// the sequence is
//
//     ClientHello
//         -> ServerCharacterList
//             -> SelectCharacterRequest | CreateCharacterRequest
//                 -> ServerWelcome
//
// with ServerReject legal in place of any server message, after which the
// connection closes. A client that receives anything else before ServerWelcome is
// talking to a peer that does not speak this protocol.
//
// **The phase is a phase and not a flag on the welcome, and the reason is
// ServerWelcome.spawn.** Where a player stands depends on which character they
// chose, so a welcome sent before the choice would carry a spawn the client must
// not trust and a correction would have to follow it. Every other field in that
// message is authoritative the moment it arrives; making one of them provisional
// would cost the message the property the rest of this contract is built on.
// ServerWelcome therefore keeps meaning exactly "you are in the world", which it
// can only mean once a character exists.
//
// **Retirement, and what a server that has not adopted V7's handshake is.** The V6
// exchange — one hello carrying ClientHello.player_token, answered directly with a
// welcome — is what every consumer in this repository still speaks: the account
// service, ticket verification and the character store are separate issues, and
// this file reserves the vocabulary ahead of them. That is the same relationship
// V6's clock had with the servers that announce `day_length_ticks = 0`: the
// contract describes the newer shape and the older one stays legible until a
// consumer adopts it.
// =============================================================================

include "common.fbs";

namespace Voxelheim.Net;

/// First message on every connection.
///
/// The client announces the protocol version it was built against, a display
/// name, and — from V7 — a signed session ticket naming the account it is
/// connecting as. The ticket is the one thing a client presents and the server
/// verifies; everything else is still assigned by the server in the reply.
/// Position, health, inventory and structures are read from the server's own
/// store for whatever identity the handshake settles on, and no client→server
/// message carries any of them, before or after this change.
///
/// **What changed at V7 is where identity comes from, not what a client may
/// state.** Through V6 the presented value was `player_token`, 32 bytes this
/// server itself minted and handed back in an earlier `ServerWelcome` — so an
/// identity existed only on the server that issued it, and a player owned nothing
/// beyond a file on their own disk. A session ticket is signed by an account
/// service instead, which is what lets one account be recognised by a server that
/// has never seen it. Presenting either one is still a *claim* and never a
/// statement: rule 4 in `schemas/AGENTS.md` is unchanged, and the server decides
/// whether the claim is honoured.
table ClientHello {
  /// Must equal `ProtocolVersion.Current` as the server understands it.
  ///
  /// Typed as the enum rather than its underlying `ushort`: the wire layout is
  /// identical, but both generated APIs then hand back a real type instead of a
  /// bare integer that could be compared against anything. An absent field
  /// decodes as `ProtocolVersion.Unknown`, which fails the check.
  protocol_version: ProtocolVersion;

  /// Display name. Untrusted, non-unique, and never used as an identifier —
  /// the server assigns `ServerWelcome.entity_id` for that.
  player_name: string;

  /// **Retired at V7.** A V7 server ignores this field and settles identity from
  /// `session_ticket` below.
  ///
  /// The field stays exactly where it is, because tags never move: removing it
  /// would renumber nothing today and would reserve a hole for ever, and a peer
  /// built against V5 or V6 still writes it. A V7 server reads past it; a V7 client
  /// has no reason to write it. Everything below this paragraph describes what the
  /// field meant while it was live, and it is kept rather than deleted because a
  /// server still resolving identity from it is speaking the **V6** handshake —
  /// which is what every consumer in this repository speaks until the account
  /// service lands. See the retirement note in this file's header.
  ///
  /// An identity token this server issued in an earlier `ServerWelcome`,
  /// presented to resume the identity it names. Absent or empty on a first
  /// connection to this server.
  ///
  /// **Asking is not stating** — rule 4 in `schemas/AGENTS.md`, the line
  /// `ChunkResendRequest` draws. Presenting a token *claims* an identity; it
  /// does not establish one. The server decides whether the claim is honoured,
  /// and everything about that identity — position, health, inventory,
  /// structures — is read from the server's own store, never from this message
  /// and never from any other client→server message. A token the server does
  /// not recognise is not a refusal: the handshake proceeds as a first
  /// connection and `ServerWelcome` carries a new token.
  ///
  /// **Decoder invariant.** Absent or empty, or **exactly 32 bytes**. Any other
  /// length is refused with `RejectReason.BAD_REQUEST` — a wrong-length token
  /// is a malformed request, decided before any identity is looked up, and the
  /// handshake is the one place a refusal has a reply payload to say so in.
  ///
  /// **A bearer credential, and what protects it is the transport rather than
  /// this contract.** Whatever can read this field can make the claim it
  /// carries. Nothing about the field changes with the transport — the same 32
  /// bytes, in the same place — so the exposure is a property of how the
  /// connection was made and not of the message:
  ///
  ///   - **The server's only configuration is encrypted.** There is no
  ///     plaintext listener and no flag to ask for one, so the field is not
  ///     readable in transit. The client verifies the server against a
  ///     certificate fingerprint it pinned, and refuses both a server that
  ///     presents a different one and a server it has an identity for but has
  ///     never pinned — so this field is never presented to a peer nobody
  ///     checked.
  ///   - **Plaintext**, for anything speaking this contract that is not this
  ///     server: anyone positioned to watch a handshake — a shared network, any
  ///     hop in between — can copy a token and come back as that player, and
  ///     nothing in this contract prevents it. The wire format cannot close
  ///     that; only the transport can.
  ///
  /// The secrecy rule is therefore stated for what it covers rather than as
  /// secrecy in general: never logged, never displayed, on either side. Those
  /// are the two exposures a wire format closes by itself, and they hold on
  /// every transport. The third — the wire — is closed by encrypting the
  /// session and by nothing a schema can say.
  player_token: [ubyte];

  /// The signed ticket naming the account this client is connecting as. **The
  /// identity half of a V7 handshake**, and the field that retires
  /// `player_token` above.
  ///
  /// Opaque here, deliberately: what is inside a ticket, who signs it and how long
  /// it is good for belong to the account service that issues it, and this contract
  /// neither parses it nor knows the signing key. What the contract fixes is its
  /// **size**, because a wrong-length ticket has to be refused before anything is
  /// looked up, and a length is the one property a decoder can check without
  /// knowing what a ticket says.
  ///
  /// **Decoder invariant.** Absent or empty, or **exactly 96 bytes**. Any other
  /// length is refused with `RejectReason.BAD_REQUEST` — a wrong-length ticket is a
  /// malformed request, decided before any account is looked up and before any
  /// signature is checked, and the handshake is the one place a refusal has a reply
  /// payload to say so in. This is deliberately the same three-way rule
  /// `player_token` carried, and the middle case means the same thing it did: a
  /// client that presents no ticket is claiming no account. Whether such a session
  /// is admitted at all is the server's admission policy and not a framing question
  /// — exactly as a `day_length_ticks` of zero is a legal announcement about a
  /// server rather than a malformed welcome.
  ///
  /// **Why 96.** A ticket is 32 bytes of body — the same width as the identity it
  /// names, and as the token it replaces — followed by a 64-byte detached Ed25519
  /// signature over that body. Neither half is read here; the split is written down
  /// so the number is a consequence of something rather than a constant somebody
  /// picked. Changing the length, the signature scheme or the split is a protocol
  /// version bump, for the reason any other change to a fixed wire shape is.
  ///
  /// **A bearer credential, exactly as `player_token` was, and protected by the
  /// same thing.** Whatever can read this field can make the claim it carries. A
  /// signature proves who *issued* the ticket, not who is presenting it, so being
  /// signed buys nothing against a copy taken off the wire. The two exposures a
  /// wire format closes by itself are closed the same way — never logged, never
  /// displayed, on either side — and the third, the wire, is closed by encrypting
  /// the session and by nothing a schema can say. This server's only configuration
  /// is encrypted. See `player_token` above for the full argument, which applies to
  /// this field unchanged.
  session_ticket: [ubyte];
}

/// Why the server refused a connection.
///
/// Wire-level error codes: they are logged verbatim on both sides and shown to
/// the player as-is, which is why they read like codes rather than prose.
enum RejectReason : ubyte {
  PROTOCOL_MISMATCH = 0,
  SERVER_FULL = 1,
  BAD_REQUEST = 2,

  /// The presented `ClientHello.player_token` names an identity that already
  /// has a live session. Distinct from `SERVER_FULL`, which is about capacity:
  /// this connection is refused however much room the server has, and a
  /// different identity would have been accepted.
  ///
  /// Appended, never reordered — the code is an integer on the wire. Renumbering
  /// the members above would silently relabel every refusal already written to a
  /// log, and a peer built against an older version reads this value as a member
  /// it has no name for.
  ALREADY_CONNECTED = 3,

  // -- V7: the three ways creating a character is refused. Appended, never
  //    inserted, for the reason ALREADY_CONNECTED records one line above.
  //
  //    They are members of *this* enum rather than of a creation-specific one
  //    because a refused `CreateCharacterRequest` is answered by `ServerReject`,
  //    which closes the connection: the handshake has not finished, so there is no
  //    session for a softer answer to live in. `ActionRefused` in `player.fbs` is
  //    the counterpart for a refusal *inside* a session, and the split between the
  //    two enums is exactly that — a connection versus an action.

  /// The name in a `CreateCharacterRequest` is already worn by a character on this
  /// world. Distinct from `CHARACTER_NAME_REFUSED`: this name is perfectly
  /// acceptable and somebody else has it, so the player retries with another and
  /// the client can say which of the two happened.
  CHARACTER_NAME_TAKEN = 4,

  /// The server will not accept the name at all — too long, too short, characters
  /// it does not allow, or a name reserved for something else. What the rule *is*
  /// belongs to the server and is deliberately not stated here; `ServerReject.detail`
  /// is where a server says which rule was broken, and it is display text that is
  /// never parsed.
  CHARACTER_NAME_REFUSED = 5,

  /// This account already holds as many characters as this world allows, and the
  /// creation would be one more. The limit itself arrives in
  /// `ServerCharacterList.max_characters`, so a client that read the list knows this
  /// refusal is coming — which is what makes it a bug report rather than news when
  /// it arrives anyway.
  CHARACTER_LIMIT_REACHED = 6
}

/// The session is accepted. Every field here is authoritative and settles a
/// parameter the client would otherwise have to guess or hardcode.
///
/// **Decoder invariants.** The client sizes buffers with these values and
/// divides by some of them, so it validates them before use and treats a
/// violation as a protocol error. A zero `tick_rate` or a wrong `chunk_size` is
/// a division by zero or an allocation blowup, and an honestly buggy server
/// reaches that state as easily as a hostile one — trusting the server on
/// gameplay outcomes does not mean trusting it on array bounds:
///
///   - `spawn` components must be finite (no NaN, no infinity)
///   - `tick_rate` must be >= 1
///   - `chunk_size` must be in 1..=40, the ceiling imposed by the 16-bit RLE run
///     length documented in `world.fbs`
///   - `view_distance` must be in 0..=16: the streamed volume grows as
///     `(2 * view_distance + 1)^3` chunks, so the bound is what keeps a single
///     number from asking the client for gigabytes
///   - `inventory_slots` and `hotbar_slots` must be non-zero
///   - `equipment_slots` must be in 1..=8, bounding the trailing equipment layout
///   - `hotbar_slots + equipment_slots` must be <= `inventory_slots`, because the
///     hotbar is the leading subset, equipment is the trailing subset, and the pack
///     occupies the slots between them
///   - `player_token` must be present and exactly 32 bytes; an absent, empty or
///     differently sized vector is a protocol error, rejected the way a zero
///     `tick_rate` is
///   - the world clock is either absent or complete. `day_length_ticks` of zero
///     says this server keeps no clock and the two boundaries below it are not
///     read at all; any other value requires
///     `0 < night_start_ticks < night_end_ticks <= day_length_ticks`, and a
///     welcome that breaks that ordering is refused rather than repaired
table ServerWelcome {
  /// Server-assigned identity for this player's entity. Appears in
  /// `EntitySnapshot` so the client can tell itself apart from everyone else.
  entity_id: ulong;

  /// Where the server has placed the player **this session**: the join spawn
  /// for an identity the server is seeing for the first time, the restored
  /// position for one that is returning. Which of the two happened is not
  /// something the client is told and not something it needs — the server
  /// decided either way. The client does not choose this and does not get to
  /// move itself there; it renders where the snapshots say.
  spawn: Vec3;

  /// Seed of the world's procedural generation. Sent for diagnostics and future
  /// client-side tooling only: the client never generates terrain from it,
  /// because the server's voxels are the only voxels that exist.
  world_seed: long;

  /// Authoritative simulation ticks per second. The client sends `PlayerInput`
  /// at this rate rather than at its frame rate.
  tick_rate: ubyte;

  /// Edge length of a chunk in blocks. Chunks are cubic, so a chunk holds
  /// `chunk_size^3` voxels. Announced once here instead of repeated in every
  /// `ChunkData`.
  chunk_size: ushort;

  /// Chunk-streaming radius in chunks, centred on the chunk the server believes
  /// the player is in.
  view_distance: ubyte;

  /// Number of slot pairs in every `InventoryState.stacks` vector this session
  /// receives. The layout is the hotbar first, the pack in the middle, and equipment
  /// last. The server currently announces 40: 9 hotbar, 27 pack and 4 equipment.
  inventory_slots: ubyte;

  /// Leading inventory slots the player's hotbar can select. The server
  /// currently announces 9.
  hotbar_slots: ubyte;

  /// The identity token for this session. The client stores it and presents it
  /// as `ClientHello.player_token` on its next connection to **this** server.
  ///
  /// **Retired at V7 alongside its counterpart**, and for the same reason: a V7
  /// client's identity comes from the account service that signed its
  /// `ClientHello.session_ticket`, not from a value this server minted. The field
  /// stays where it is and every invariant below still holds for the value a server
  /// puts in it — present, 32 bytes, never the value the client sent unless the
  /// server chose it — because a V6 client on the far end still stores what arrives
  /// here and a V7 server has no reason to send a shorter one. What is retired is
  /// the *sentence above*: presenting this value on a later connection is the V6
  /// handshake, and a V7 server ignores it.
  ///
  /// Present and exactly 32 bytes on every accepted handshake; a decoder treats
  /// any other length as a protocol error, the way it treats a zero `tick_rate`.
  ///
  /// A client whose presented token the server did not recognise receives a
  /// **new** token here rather than the value it sent: the server never adopts a
  /// client-chosen value as an identity key, so every token in circulation is
  /// one the server minted. A client whose token *was* recognised stores
  /// whatever arrives here, which is not required to be the value it sent.
  ///
  /// The same bearer credential, carrying the same three exposures and closing
  /// them the same way. Two are the wire format's own and hold on every
  /// transport: never logged, never displayed, on either side. The third is the
  /// wire itself, and it is closed by the transport rather than by anything a
  /// schema can say — this server's only configuration is encrypted, so the
  /// field is not readable in transit. See `ClientHello.player_token`.
  player_token: [ubyte];

  /// Length of one full day in server ticks, or **zero for a server that keeps no
  /// clock at all**.
  ///
  /// Zero is not a degenerate day. It is the world as it was before V6, in which
  /// time does not pass and a client draws whatever fixed sky it draws — a legal,
  /// expected value, and the only one that makes the two boundaries below
  /// meaningless. Every other value is a promise about
  /// `EntitySnapshot.tick_of_day`, which is then always less than it.
  ///
  /// Ticks rather than seconds, for the reason every other duration on this wire
  /// is in ticks: the simulation counts them, and integer arithmetic gives both
  /// sides the same answer without a rounding rule to agree on.
  day_length_ticks: uint;

  /// The tick of the day at which night begins.
  ///
  /// Read only when `day_length_ticks` is non-zero, and then
  /// `0 < night_start_ticks < night_end_ticks <= day_length_ticks`.
  ///
  /// **Sent rather than left for the client to derive.** A client could compute a
  /// plausible dusk from constants of its own, and the night it drew would then be
  /// its night rather than the server's — which is the night the server's own rules
  /// are written against. There is one clock in this world and these are its
  /// boundaries; a second opinion about them is a disagreement waiting to be seen
  /// on screen.
  night_start_ticks: uint;

  /// The tick of the day at which night ends. Read under the same condition and the
  /// same ordering as `night_start_ticks`, and equal to `day_length_ticks` for a
  /// night that runs to the end of the day.
  night_end_ticks: uint;

  /// Trailing inventory slots reserved for worn equipment. These slots are part of
  /// `inventory_slots`, and the server currently announces four: head, chest, legs and
  /// off-hand, in that slot order at the inventory tail. The client uses this count to
  /// find where the pack ends; it never decides which item is legal in a worn slot.
  equipment_slots: ubyte;
}

/// The session is refused. The connection closes immediately after this.
table ServerReject {
  reason: RejectReason;

  /// Human-readable detail for logs and for the client's status text. Never
  /// parsed — branch on `reason`, display `detail`.
  detail: string;
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// V7 — choosing a character, before there is a world to stand in.
//
// These three messages sit between ClientHello and ServerWelcome. None of them is
// a gameplay message and none of them is legal after the welcome: a peer that
// receives one out of phase treats it as a protocol error, exactly as it treats a
// payload arriving from the wrong direction.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One character an account owns on this world, as `ServerCharacterList` lists it.
///
/// Everything needed to draw a row in a character-select screen and nothing else.
/// There is no position, no health, no inventory and no play time: those are the
/// world's answer to "who is this", read from the server's own store once the
/// character has been chosen, and a list that carried them would be handing out
/// state before an identity had been settled.
///
/// A table rather than a struct, for the reason `Appearance` is one and for one
/// more: it holds a string, which a struct cannot.
///
/// Decoder invariants:
///   - `character_id` is never 0. Zero names no character anywhere in this
///     contract, so a `SelectCharacterRequest` carrying it is refused rather than
///     read as the first row
///   - `name` is present and non-empty. A character with no name is a store that
///     has lost one, not a character
///   - `appearance` is present and satisfies every invariant `Appearance`
///     documents. The client draws this character and never invents a default
table CharacterSummary {
  /// Server-minted, stable for the life of the character, and unique within this
  /// world. **Not an entity id**: an entity id names a body in a running
  /// simulation and is forgotten when the session ends, while this outlives every
  /// session the character ever has. `ServerWelcome.entity_id` is what the chosen
  /// character becomes once it is in the world.
  character_id: ulong;

  /// The character's name, as the player chose it and the server accepted it.
  /// Display text: shown, never parsed, and never used as an identifier — that is
  /// what `character_id` is for.
  name: string;

  /// What this character looks like. See `Appearance` in `common.fbs`, which is
  /// authoritative for the colour encoding.
  appearance: Appearance;
}

/// Every character this account owns **on this world**, sent in answer to a
/// `ClientHello` the server is willing to continue with.
///
/// **On this world**, and that is the whole scope of the message: an account may own
/// characters on other servers and this one neither knows nor says. A server answers
/// for its own store.
///
/// An empty `characters` vector is a legal and expected answer — a new account, or
/// one that has never played here — and it is not a refusal. It says the only way
/// forward is `CreateCharacterRequest`, which is exactly what a client with an empty
/// list can offer.
///
/// Decoder invariants:
///   - `characters` may be empty but is never absent; an absent vector and an empty
///     one are read the same way, as "no characters here"
///   - no `character_id` appears twice
///   - every summary satisfies the invariants `CharacterSummary` documents
///   - `max_characters` is non-zero, and at least the length of `characters`. A
///     server that already holds more characters than it says it allows is a server
///     disagreeing with itself, and a client that quietly took the larger of the two
///     would be inventing a limit nobody set
table ServerCharacterList {
  /// The characters, in the order the server chose to present them. That order is
  /// the server's and carries no meaning the client may read — it is not recency,
  /// not creation order and not rank — so a client that wants a particular order
  /// sorts by something it can see.
  characters: [CharacterSummary];

  /// How many characters this account may hold on this world, including the ones
  /// above. Sent rather than left for the client to hardcode, for the reason every
  /// other limit in `ServerWelcome` is: the number belongs to the server, and a
  /// client that guessed it would offer or refuse a creation the server disagrees
  /// with. A creation attempted past it is `RejectReason.CHARACTER_LIMIT_REACHED`.
  max_characters: ubyte;
}

/// The client chooses which existing character is playing this session.
/// **Client -> server, and a claim rather than a statement.**
///
/// It names an id the server minted and sent in `ServerCharacterList`, which is the
/// one kind of identifier a client may echo back — the same rule
/// `RemoveStructureRequest` follows. Whether the id names a character *this account*
/// owns is re-read from the server's own store, so naming somebody else's gains
/// nothing.
///
/// The server answers with `ServerWelcome` or with `ServerReject`. There is
/// deliberately no member of `RejectReason` for "no such character": a selection
/// naming an id this account does not own is `BAD_REQUEST`, decided the way a
/// wrong-length ticket is, because telling a client apart "that character does not
/// exist" from "that character is not yours" would let anyone enumerate the
/// world's characters by asking.
///
/// Decoder invariant: `character_id` is copied verbatim, zero and unknown ids
/// included, and refused by the server — whether an id names a character this
/// account may play is a decision, not a framing question.
table SelectCharacterRequest {
  character_id: ulong;
}

/// The client asks for a new character. **Client -> server, and intent only.**
///
/// It carries a name and a face, which is everything a player chooses about a
/// character and nothing a player decides about the world. There is no
/// `character_id` here — an id is minted by the server when a character comes into
/// existence, and a client that could name one could name one that already exists.
/// There is no position, no health and no inventory either: what a new character
/// starts with is the server's answer, exactly as a returning character's is.
///
/// The server answers with `ServerWelcome` — creation and selection are one step, so
/// a created character is the character playing this session — or with
/// `ServerReject` carrying `CHARACTER_NAME_TAKEN`, `CHARACTER_NAME_REFUSED` or
/// `CHARACTER_LIMIT_REACHED`.
///
/// Decoder invariants, on untrusted input:
///   - `name` is copied verbatim, the empty string and a name of any length
///     included. What names a server accepts is a *decision* and this contract does
///     not state the rule; a refusal is `CHARACTER_NAME_REFUSED`, which is an answer
///     with a reply, where a decode error would close the connection with nothing
///     said. This is the division of labour `ClientHello.player_name` already
///     follows
///   - `appearance` is present and satisfies every invariant `Appearance` documents.
///     Unlike the name, this one *is* a framing question: an absent appearance or an
///     `Unknown` hair model is not a value the server can refuse politely, it is a
///     request that failed to say what it is asking for
table CreateCharacterRequest {
  /// The name the player has chosen. Untrusted display text, and never an
  /// identifier — `CharacterSummary.character_id` is what identifies a character
  /// once the server has minted one.
  name: string;

  /// What the new character looks like. See `Appearance` in `common.fbs`.
  appearance: Appearance;
}
